  "review": "# Atomic probe review (offline only)\n\nThe final six-entry source and all four O1/O2 gfx1013/gfx1030 objects in\n`results/g3-atomics-direct` are ready to promote as compiler/research fixtures.\nNo actionable lowering error was found. This is not native or GPU qualification.\nThe original `g3-atomics-probe` pilot remains independently recorded in\n`inspection.json`; `direct-inspection.json` binds the strengthened final objects.\nThe intermediate `g3-atomics-wide` variant was not separately re-inspected here.\n\n## Lowering and entry requirements\n\n- Original and final `atomic_ticket32/64` use wave aggregation: EXEC population\n  count and lane rank, one elected atomic add returning a wave reservation,\n  broadcast and per-lane rank addition. With 257 groups of 64 wave32 threads,\n  that is 514 contending reservations producing 16,448 logical tickets, not\n  16,448 independent hardware add operations. O1/O2 and both targets agree.\n- Final `atomic_indirect32/64` load each lane's runtime offset, compute a full\n  address and execute `global_atomic_add`/`global_atomic_add_x2` without EXEC\n  election, population-count aggregation or first-lane broadcast. Initializing\n  all 16,448 offsets to zero gives direct per-lane contention at one counter.\n  Preserve and check the entire offsets input after completion. No compiler\n  toggle or special lowering is needed for this inspected varying-address form.\n- CAS is per lane, strong (source `weak=false`), expected value zero. The original\n  u64 pilot desired `u64(id)+1` never exercises nonzero upper words; it cannot\n  detect high-word truncation. Final CAS64 puts `0x13579bdf` in the desired high\n  VGPR, id+1 in its low VGPR, and zero in both compare VGPRs before\n  `global_atomic_cmpswap_x2`. Returned old memory is stored as a full dword pair.\n  Final CAS32 similarly supplies desired id+1 and compare zero.\n- All 24 final descriptors request six user SGPRs, properties0x409, RSRC2=0x8c,\n  RSRC3=0, private/group bytes0. Kernargs are16 bytes for ticket/CAS or24 for\n  indirect (two/three explicit global pointers, offsets0/8[/16], align8).\n  Metadata is wave32, max workgroup1024, no hidden args, dynamic stack or spills.\n  Entry instructions consume s4:s5 kernarg, s6 groupX and v0 localX; no scratch\n  initialization, calls or private traffic was observed. Keep64x1x1 explicitly.\n  This matches the existing structural entry profile; it does not certify atomics.\n- Each atomic has `glc` and a destination for the returned old value. The saved\n  ISA waits for vmcnt before using/storing the result; indirect paths also wait\n  for the loaded offset. Aggregated paths restore EXEC and wait before broadcast.\n  Preserve the emitted target-specific waits (including gfx1013 depctr).\n  Neither these waits nor `glc` prove CPU/GPU cache coherence or system scope.\n\n## Exact output oracle\n\n`oracle.py` fixes the invocation count at16,448 and validates unsigned widths\nwithout accepting bools, truncating integers or reducing erroneous input modulo\n2^width. Ticket initial value is2^width-4096; final must be12,352. Every observed\nold value must map uniquely to one offset in[0,16448) from that initial value,\nmodulo the selected width. Any permutation is accepted; duplicates, omissions,\nout-of-range tickets and incorrect final values fail. The u64 case crosses the\nfull64-bit wrap and catches32-bit truncation even though its final value is small.\n\nFor CAS, final must be an invocation token. Token base is0 for CAS32 and the\noriginal64-bit pilot; final64-bit qualification uses0x13579bdf00000000. Exactly\none observation is zero, at invocation(final-token_base-1). All other observations\nmust equal the complete final token. Any winner is valid; no thread ordering is\nassumed. Strong CAS on an initially zero counter with distinct nonzero desired\nvalues has one success; subsequent failures return that unchanged winner.\n\n`validate_indirect` applies the same ticket oracle and checks exactly16,448\nunchanged zero offsets. Native qualification must additionally check counter and\noutput guards, full allocation extents, input initialization, natural4/8-byte\ncounter alignment, retained ownership, completion/readback and clean teardown.\nSynthetic arrays here deliberately establish none of those properties.\n\n## Scope and verification\n\nSource requests `__ATOMIC_RELAXED` on GCC-style builtins with no explicit scope\nargument. The saved experiment did not emit LLVM IR, so this review makes no new\nIR-syncscope claim. The proposed experiment observes contention among GPU\ninvocations within one dispatch after the existing completion/readback path.\nIt does not qualify acquire/release, sequential consistency, concurrent host\natomics, system-wide coherency, cross-dispatch publication, misaligned atomics,\nother widths, general progress or physical CU coverage.\n\nThe review rehashed original and final source/recipes plus40 saved artifacts,\nchecked40 kernel descriptors and both sets of actual opcode/entry behavior,\nand resolved all8 driver linker paths to LLVM18. Final inspections can be replayed\nwith `python3 -B inspect_saved.py`; it reads saved files only. Thirteen synthetic\nmutation-test methods passed via `python3 -B test_oracle.py`: wrong finals,\nduplicate/lost/out-of-range tickets, u64 truncation, inconsistent CAS winner,\nmissing/multiple successes, bogus observations, changed offsets and malformed\ninput types/extents. Positive tests include shuffled/reversed ticket orders and\nseveral arbitrary winners. `validation.json` binds commands and raw logs.\nNo production code, compiler rerun or console operation was performed.\n",
